Challenges Faced
Planning Stage

    Choosing and Implementing Complex Data Structures: Deciding on suitable structures like the red-black tree and trie was challenging, as they required balancing functionality with performance. Planning for efficient retrieval and insertion, especially for the high-score feature, required extensive analysis. Implementing an undo feature for moves required careful consideration of the linked list structure.
    Securing User Data with Hashing: Security considerations were paramount, especially without relying on external libraries. Planning how to hash and store passwords in a secure and manageable way was complex, given the risks of hash collisions and vulnerabilities.
    Creating an Intuitive Interface: Designing a system that addresses players by usernames and uses numbers 1-9 for move positions required careful planning to enhance usability while keeping the code efficient and straightforward.

Coding Stage

    Memory Management in Dynamic Data Structures: Managing memory allocation and deallocation, especially with linked structures like the linked list for moves and the trie for move sequences, required attention to avoid memory leaks.
    Collision Handling in Hash Map: Collisions in the hash map posed challenges, particularly in managing multiple users with similar usernames. The code uses linear probing to address these collisions.
    Red-Black Tree Balancing: Coding the red-black tree balancing mechanisms, including rotations and color adjustments, was complex. Ensuring the tree remained balanced after insertions and deletions was essential.
    Optimizing AI Performance: The Minimax algorithm needed optimization to avoid performance issues and keep gameplay responsive.
    File I/O for Persistent Storage: Implementing file I/O to save and retrieve user data and high scores introduced challenges related to data serialization.

Comparison to Basic Implementations

This implementation stands out from basic Tic-Tac-Toe applications due to its advanced features and use of complex data structures:

    User Management and Persistence: Basic versions lack user accounts, password security, and data persistence. This application securely manages multiple users and saves high scores across sessions.
    AI with Minimax: Simple Tic-Tac-Toe games typically do not feature an intelligent AI. Here, the Minimax-based AI presents a real challenge, making the PvC mode engaging and competitive.
    Enhanced High-Score System: Instead of an unsorted or static list, the red-black tree maintains a dynamically updated, sorted list of players based on wins.
    Advanced Move Tracking and Undo Feature: The linked list implementation for move tracking and the trie for move sequences introduce complexity and depth, supporting undo functionality and move sequence storage.

Conclusion

This Tic-Tac-Toe application in C demonstrates a comprehensive approach to game and data management through sophisticated data structures, security features, and user interface design. While the code presents challenges, particularly in terms of memory management, security, and efficiency, its feature set and extensibility make it a unique and advanced application.

