fsm_action_demo

A ROS2 (rclpy) action server that behaves like a goal state machine, plus 5 action client nodes simulating different sensors. Uses the pre-existing example_interfaces/action/Fibonacci interface — no custom .action file to define or build, so you can verify it immediately with ros2 interface show example_interfaces/action/Fibonacci.
Layout

fsm_action_demo/
├── package.xml
├── setup.py
├── setup.cfg
├── resource/fsm_action_demo
└── fsm_action_demo/
    ├── fsm_server.py          # the FSM action server
    ├── sensor_client_base.py  # shared client logic
    ├── gps_client.py          # node1
    ├── ice_client.py          # node2
    ├── imu_client.py          # node3
    ├── lava_client.py         # node4
    ├── airlock_client.py      # node5
    └── mission_sequencer.py   # drives the exact required goal order

Build

mkdir -p ~/ros2_ws/src
cp -r fsm_action_demo ~/ros2_ws/src/
cd ~/ros2_ws
colcon build --symlink-install --packages-select fsm_action_demo
source install/setup.bash

Run

Terminal 1 — start the FSM server:

ros2 run fsm_action_demo fsm_server

Terminal 2 — run the full required mission order in one go (GPS, ice, GPS, IMU, lava, GPS, airlock), each waiting for the previous goal's result before the next is sent:

ros2 run fsm_action_demo mission_sequencer

Or run any individual client (each is its own node):

ros2 run fsm_action_demo gps_client --ros-args -p value:=42
ros2 run fsm_action_demo ice_client
ros2 run fsm_action_demo imu_client
ros2 run fsm_action_demo lava_client
ros2 run fsm_action_demo airlock_client

(Command-line args --value 0-100 and --cancel-after <seconds> are also accepted directly, e.g. ros2 run fsm_action_demo gps_client --value 80.)
Demonstrating REJECT

Launch 4 clients back-to-back within the same second (queue cap is 3):

ros2 run fsm_action_demo gps_client &
ros2 run fsm_action_demo ice_client &
ros2 run fsm_action_demo imu_client &
ros2 run fsm_action_demo lava_client &   # this one gets REJECTed, queue is full

Demonstrating QUEUEING

Launch 2 clients back-to-back — the second is ACCEPTed but logs show it waiting behind the first before its feedback starts flowing:

ros2 run fsm_action_demo gps_client &
ros2 run fsm_action_demo ice_client &

Demonstrating CANCEL

ros2 run fsm_action_demo gps_client --cancel-after 4

The client sends a cancel request 4 seconds after its goal is accepted; watch the server log the goal being cancelled mid-execution.
Verifying the interface

ros2 interface show example_interfaces/action/Fibonacci
ros2 action list
ros2 action info /sensor_data

Encoding scheme

example_interfaces/Fibonacci only has one input field (order), so each goal packs two things into it:

order = sensor_id * 1000 + test_value      # test_value in 0-100
sensor_id: 1=GPS, 2=ice, 3=imu, 4=lava tube, 5=airlock

Feedback/result sequence is the value ramping from 0 up to test_value across 11 steps (~10 seconds at 1 Hz), mirroring the "0-10 over 10 seconds" requirement while still allowing a distinguishable 0-100 test value per goal.
